                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A surprisingly diverse and entertaining collection of three little gems from the talented team that gave us such masterpieces as \"A Tale of Two Kingdoms\" and \"Heroine's Quest\".\nYes, it is a bit of irony that their big games are completely free, while the small games do cost a little. Still, it is finally a chance to express some gratitude to the developers - and at that, 'Crystal Shard Adventure Bundle' is totally worth its price! \n\nHere we have:\n\n1) 'Quest for Yrolg' - it was the most interesting one for me: a kind of mix of classic point-and-click adventure game and, well, 'set-traps-and-lure-enemies-into-them' puzzle not unlike the classical 'Home Alone' game! Our inventory is limited with only one item, but the gameplay is non-linear, and invloves as many as six parts. First, we set traps for each of the three heroes we have to defeat, and then see them falling for our tricks. Quite an amusement!\n\n2) 'Starship Quasar' - now, that's more classical point-and-click adventure game, but the surprise here is that we have four persons to play as, gaining access to them one by one, while trying to make them all friends again. Probably the only rather serious game in the collection, but still very fun! A robocat is included.\n\n3) 'Larry Lotter and the Test of Time' - well, I am not at all familiar with that 'Harry Potter' character, so I may miss the whole fun of this parody. That's my least favorite game of the three; still, it has its own load of nice features like 'reversing time' and other magical spells that could and should be used.\n\nThe only complain is that the quality of localizations (at least, that of Russian) is unfortunately far from being excellent - it would be better to play in English.\n\nIn total, that was about four hours for me with different styles and subgenres - and probably one of the most memorable adventure experiences of 2016!",
